# scaleup_3tier_256_4000Gbps.topo
#
# A 3-TIER scale-up fat-tree of 256 hosts, minted for the multicast bandwidth-
# usage-reduction (network-footprint) experiment (simulation-scripts/experiments/
# scaleup_coll_footprint). Its job is to expose the Ring-vs-Recursive-Doubling
# footprint divergence, which is a MULTI-TIER effect: on a single switch every
# pair is 2 hops so Ring and RD are identical; only a tree with several hop-
# distance regimes lets RD's large late-step messages travel farther and diverge.
#
# WHY 3 TIERS (not 2): a 2-tier tree has only two hop regimes (2 hops intra-leaf,
# 4 hops cross-leaf), so RD saturates as soon as the group exceeds one leaf. A
# 3-tier tree adds a third regime (6 hops inter-pod) so RD keeps climbing with the
# group size P — reproducing the paper's shape (Khalilov et al. SC24, Fig. 2, whose
# own topology is a radix-32 THREE-tier fat-tree; 2-tier radix-32 caps at 512).
#
# HOP-DISTANCE REGIMES (contiguous rank->host placement, host i = rank i):
#   same leaf   (|i-j| < 4)        : host -> leaf -> host                 = 2 hops
#   same pod    (4 <= |i-j| < 16)  : host -> leaf -> agg -> leaf -> host   = 4 hops
#   diff pod    (|i-j| >= 16)      : host -> leaf -> agg -> core -> ...    = 6 hops
# So a group of size P sweeps: P<=16 stays intra-pod (RD ~= Ring); P>=32 spans
# pods and RD begins to diverge; P in {64,128,256} = 4/8/16 pods.
#
# STRUCTURE (4 hosts/leaf x 4 leaves/pod x 16 pods = 256; all switches < 96 ports):
#   Tier 0 (leaf): Radix_Down 4 (hosts), Radix_Up 4.  #leaves = 256/4 = 64. ports 8.
#   Tier 1 (agg) : Radix_Down 4 (leaves/pod), Radix_Up 4. agg/pod = 4; #agg = 16x4 = 64. ports 8.
#   Tier 2 (core): Radix_Down 16 (= #pods).  #core = leaf_up x agg_up = 4x4 = 16. ports 16.
#   Full bisection / 1:1 oversubscription at every tier (up-bw == down-bw).
#
# BANDWIDTH / LATENCY: footprint (link-crosses) is INDEPENDENT of link speed and
# latency, so these values do not affect the metric. Downlink_speed_Gbps 4000 matches
# the sibling scaleup_single_switch_64_4000Gbps.topo: 4000 Gbps is exactly 2 ps/B, so
# the fabric rate equals the receiver-NIC rate (-intranode_linkspeed 4000000) by
# construction instead of via integer-ps rounding (which maps 3600 -> 2 ps/B too).
# Idealized 500 ns link / 300 ns switch (as in the SUE nonblocking file) for
# cross-topology comparability.
Nodes 256
Tiers 3
Podsize 16

Tier 0
Downlink_speed_Gbps 4000
Radix_Down 4
Radix_Up 4
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
Oversubscribed 1

Tier 1
Downlink_speed_Gbps 4000
Radix_Down 4
Radix_Up 4
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
Oversubscribed 1

Tier 2
Downlink_speed_Gbps 4000
Radix_Down 16
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
